<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need Two Papayas, Please. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Two Papayas, Please.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences. Reading: Extract specific information from texts on familiar topics. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences at a farm stand (e.g., "I go to the market and see some fruit."). Can extract specific information from texts about farm products (e.g., "Papayas are on sale."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes, using knowledge of syllable segmentation (e.g., segmenting "banana" into "ba-na-na" with three syllables).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of fruits and vegetables introduced in Lesson 1 (e.g., papaya, banana, mango, pineapple, tomato, pepper, lettuce, egg, bread, cheese). Students collectively name the items. The teacher asks, "Do you like [papaya]? Where can we buy [papayas]?" This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and connects to the scenario of a farm stand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models pronunciation of key vocabulary from the flashcards, emphasizing /p/ in "papaya" and /b/ in "banana." The teacher demonstrates a simple request, "Can I have some papayas, please?" pointing to a picture, and then models a simple reading of a word, e.g., pointing to "banana" on a word strip and reading it aloud, then clapping syllables: "ba-na-na" (3 syllables).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and simple requests.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a papaya or a banana?" (showing pictures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Please,' am I being polite or not polite?" (referring to requests).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text (approx. 50-70 words) titled "A Visit to the Farm Stand" presented on a large chart, projector, or individual handouts. The text includes core vocabulary (papaya, banana, mango, eggs, bread, milk, market, cashier, price), grammar (polite requests, WH-questions, some/any), and mentions the phonemes /p/ and /b/ and words for syllable segmentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b><br/>"Maria goes to the local farm stand. She sees some big, fresh papayas. 'Hello!' she says to the cashier. 'How much are the papayas, please?' The cashier says, 'They are one dollar each.' Maria asks, 'Do you have any fresh bananas?' The cashier replies, 'Yes, we have some delicious bananas.' Maria buys two papayas and three bananas. She needs some eggs too, but the farm stand doesn't have any eggs today. She also wants some fresh bread and milk."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend short narratives about familiar experiences and extract specific information from texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Reading - Gist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud, modeling fluency. Students listen and follow along silently.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Where does Maria go in the story?" (Students can point to a picture of a farm stand or say "farm stand" or "market").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Maria buys some mangoes." (False) "Maria sees some papayas." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, extracting specific information, decoding words with /p/ and /b/, segmenting syllables. Grammar focus: "How much...?", "Do you have any...?", "We have some...", "I don't have any...". Vocabulary: farm stand products.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Reading Practice & Phonemic Awareness:</b> Students read the "A Visit to the Farm Stand" text aloud in chorus, then individually. The teacher guides them to identify and circle all words with the /p/ sound (e.g., 'papayas', 'pepper', 'pineapple') and /b/ sound (e.g., 'bananas', 'bread'). The teacher then asks students to clap the syllables for words like "papaya" (pa-pa-ya) and "banana" (ba-na-na), focusing on segmentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Information Extraction (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences based on the text. They read the text again to find the missing information and complete the sentences. Example: "Maria needs two ______." "The farm stand doesn't have any ______ today." "The papayas are ______ dollar each." This practices extracting specific details.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Question Matching:</b> In pairs, students receive two sets of cards: one with simple WH-questions about the text (e.g., "How many papayas did Maria buy?", "What else does Maria want?") and another with corresponding answers from the text. Students take turns reading a question card and finding the correct answer card, practicing both reading comprehension and identifying specific information. They also practice asking "How much...?" and "Do you have any...?" if the questions are structured around availability and price.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Read and Complete the Farm Stand Order." Students work individually to read short descriptions of customer orders at the farm stand and then complete a simple order form or drawing based on what they read. This focuses on reading comprehension and extracting specific details for a practical output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Each student receives a worksheet with 2-3 short, distinct customer requests or scenarios at a farm stand (e.g., "Customer A needs three ripe mangoes and some fresh lettuce. They also ask for the price of milk. The farm stand has no guava today." "Customer B wants one big pineapple and two red tomatoes. They ask if there is any bread."). Students read each scenario and then complete a simple shopping list or draw the items each customer wants based *only* on the text provided. They also indicate if an item is not available. They must also identify numbers and prices mentioned (e.g., "The mangoes are two dollars each.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading, extracting specific numbers/items, understanding availability ("some" / "any") and polite requests, and accurately transferring information from text to a visual or written product.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation checklist during production task and a short comprehension quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short reading comprehension quiz based on a *new but similar* short text or a slightly modified version of the "A Visit to the Farm Stand" narrative. The quiz includes:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Multiple-choice questions to identify main idea or specific details.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False statements about facts presented in the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A question requiring them to underline words with /p/ or /b/ sounds, or to circle words with three syllables (e.g., "banana," "papaya").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to extract specific information from the text (e.g., number of items, availability).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of the narrative's main points.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of target phonemes and syllable segmentation (if included in quiz).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about going to the market and buying fruit: Yes / A little / No" (Students circle one).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing you learned from reading today's story?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when reading the story today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the story was difficult for me to understand?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson (e.g., specific activity, text clarity, student engagement)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with comprehension or phonemic awareness? (e.g., more visual aids, simpler texts, re-teaching specific vocabulary or grammar points).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson provided the contextual and linguistic foundation (vocabulary, grammar, scenario) for students to actively produce language. In Lesson 3 (Speaking), students will use the knowledge gained from reading the farm stand narrative to engage in simple conversations and role-plays about buying and selling items at "The Local Farm Stand," utilizing the target vocabulary and polite requests introduced and practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>